{"name":"Lichen Green","hex":"#9da693","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lichen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many symbiotic organisms, being associations of algae and fungi, often found as white or yellow-to-blue–green patches on rocks, old walls, etc.","Something which gradually spreads across something else, causing damage."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover with lichen."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lichen"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lichen","blocks":[{"text":"A lichen is a hybrid colony of algae or cyanobacteria living symbiotically among filaments of multiple fungus species, along with bacteria embedded in the cortex, or \"skin\", in a mutualistic relationship. Lichens are the lifeform that first brought the term symbiosis into biological context."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6e/Letharia_vulpina_JHollinger_crop.jpg/330px-Letharia_vulpina_JHollinger_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lichen","wikidata":"Q43142"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg/500px-Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg/1280px-Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg/500px-Flavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_%287%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlavoparmelia_caperata_-_Flickr_-_pellaea_(7).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Caloplaca_marina.JPG/500px-Caloplaca_marina.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Caloplaca_marina.JPG/1280px-Caloplaca_marina.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikime